WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.970
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.490
So in this lecture,
I'd like you to have

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.980
a first connection
between particle physics

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.540
and the Lagrangian formalism.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.190
In classical mechanics,
you have seen

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.090
that you can write down
the Lagrangian using

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.700
the kinetic and the potential
energy of a particle,

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.660
and from that derive
equations of motions.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.610
In quantum field theory,
you can translate this idea

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:34.630
and derive Lagrangian densities.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.370
It's beyond the
scope of this class

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.770
to do all the mechanics of this.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:43.210
We'll visit this topic
later in the class

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.410
when we introduce the Higgs
mechanism, for example.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:49.720
And we'll be a little
bit more systematic then.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.320
I'm introducing the topic now
because it allows you to answer

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.700
one of the homework questions.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.650
So you can just
follow this lecture

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.850
and then you should be able
to answer the first question

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.010
of the second p-set.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:08.670
All right, so you just have
to trust me at this point

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.090
that you can write the
Lagrangian for a Dirac field,

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:16.710
or Lagrangian density for
a Dirac field this way.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.860
One exercise would be
to use this and show

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.210
that from this Lagrangian you
can derive the Dirac equation

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.547
for a spinor field.

00:01:26.547 --> 00:01:28.380
But that's not what
we're trying to do here.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.720
You're trying to see
what's the effect is

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.850
of having this Lagrange density
being invariant or unchanged

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.780
under global symmetry.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.780
So we are able to
rotate our spinor

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.620
field with a global phase.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.810
And we will see
that the Lagrangian

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.030
doesn't change and the
consequence of this,

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.290
which is if we can.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.730
This is exercising
Noether's theory.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.470
There's an overarching
global symmetry.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:59.580
And out of the symmetry follows
the conserved property--

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.410
in this case, the current.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:06.150
All right, so we can
express the symmetry

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.360
with infinitesimal
phase transformation,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.770
as shown here, for our fields
and for our adjunct fields.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.900
For the field and
the derivatives,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.130
then, you just
have to do the math

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:25.830
and we find those
expressions, which we can then

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.920
put back into our Lagrangian.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.630
First of all, we
write the change

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.360
of our Lagrangian in this way.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.540
And as we just have
seen the Lagrangian,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.380
its invariant under
this transformation,

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.890
and therefore the
change is going to be 0.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.960
So then we use this information
at this in the equations.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.530
We find this very
complicated-looking set

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:50.430
of equations.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.050
OK, so now we get this.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.398
And then we can
rewrite the terms.

00:02:58.398 --> 00:03:00.440
So this is already with
a vision of what we would

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:03.470
like to actually find later.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:13.570
So if we now look at the
terms involving the derivative

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:19.480
with respect to du
mu of our spinor,

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.140
we can express this
equation as shown here.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.080
And with that, we find
the next equation.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.310
I only show this for the spinor
not for the adjunct spinor.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.230
This looks exactly the same.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.870
But you, however, find
in this part here,

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:38.720
this looks like
Euler-Lagrange equation.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.640
And this part needs to be 0.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.120
So we only have to worry about
this part of the equation,

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:46.090
and the same for
the adjunct field.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:49.040
So this then leaves
this equation here

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.240
where we have i
epsilon, a derivative

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.610
of this part of the equation.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:59.990
And something like this you have
seen before in our continuity

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:04.200
equation, something like this.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.590
It's our continuity equation.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.890
We discussed this in one
of the last recitation

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.760
session, which leads us
then to conserve currents.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.800
And let's go one step further.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:21.630
If we now identify this
part as our current,

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.350
we can then use the
partial derivatives

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.060
of our initial Lagrangian.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.880
Now we're just calculating
those terms here.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.800
And we find that our current,
our conserved current

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.680
is given by the adjunct
spinor, gamma mu spinor.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.030
And so what we have just
seen-- and this is conserved,

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.720
so the derivative is 0--

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.580
have seen that we have
a Lagrangian density,

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.310
we have a global symmetry.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:55.145
And out of that, we find that
the current is conserved.

00:04:55.145 --> 00:04:59.580
So this is all I wanted to show
here in the homework set now.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.380
We start from a
different Lagrangian.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.010
So this is our Lagrangian
for a massive spin half

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:08.880
particle, which satisfies
the Dirac equation.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.140
In the homework, we are
looking at a scalar particle,

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.790
a massive scalar particle.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:19.010
And the exercise, however,
is very much the same.

